      'From the spring the chair casts you each term in one of the School’s productions: the Carlotta Festival as a first-year; a directors’ thesis or the Langston Hughes Festival in a fall after that, and a thesis or the Carlotta Festival in the second spring; a Shakespeare Repertory Project in your last spring. Once a year, in good standing, you’re at Yale Rep too, most often understudying. On call, the Rep calls at five on each performance day: be in New Haven then, and the evening is yours unless you’re needed, when you’re told and the performance goes on your calendar. Every understudy plays one run-through for the director. The chair casts each play by what it asks of your skills: the best read takes the biggest part written for them, a lead counts against your next lead and a small part for it, and how your last run’s nights went counts too. Each night is off, solid, strong or remarkable, by how rested you come, your nerves and how the part sits on your craft, and the director gives a note at the end of the run. Miss two calls in a term, labs and rehearsals together, and you’re out of the casting pool until a good evaluation. A missed performance, or not being in New Haven when you’re called to go on, is a grave breach: it puts you on academic warning at once, and on warning it dismisses you.',
